<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cloves">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cloves</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cloves are the aromatic dried flower buds of a tree native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. They were among the most valuable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> of the Asian trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> competed for control of their production, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> enforcing a strict monopoly.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cloves.jpg" alt="Cloves" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syzygium_aromaticum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-030.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Cloves are the aromatic flower buds of Syzygium aromaticum, an evergreen tree of the family Myrtaceae that grows up to 8 to 12 metres tall, with large leaves and crimson flowers grouped in terminal clusters. The buds are pale at first, turn green and then bright red when ready for harvest, and are picked at 1.5 to 2 centimetres in length. The tree is native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a>, or Moluccas, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. The word clove first appears in English in the 15th century and derives, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> clou de girofle, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> clavus, meaning nail. A major component of the taste is the chemical eugenol, which makes up 72 to 90 per cent of the essential oil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Until the colonial era cloves grew only on a few islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>, historically called the Spice Islands, among them Bacan, Makian, Moti, Ternate and Tidore. They were first traded by the Austronesian peoples in their maritime trade network, which began around 1500 BCE and later became the Maritime Silk Road and part of the Spice Trade. The archaeologist Giorgio Buccellati found cloves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terqa">Terqa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, in a burned house dated to 1720 BCE, the first evidence of their use in the west before Roman times. They reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by the first century CE.</p><p>A Chinese book of about 200 BCE, the Han Guan Yi, says that ministers should suck cloves to sweeten their breath before speaking to the emperor. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> private ventures imported cloves from Austronesian polities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> and Butuan, and during the Yuan dynasty Chinese merchants began sending ships directly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>. Some early mentions of cloves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> from before Southeast Asian maritime trade are thought to be misidentifications of other plants such as cassia buds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Cloves flavour meats, curries, marinades, fruit and hot drinks, and are common in spice blends such as the Malay rempah empat beradik. They are added to betel quids, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> they are used in a type of cigarette called kretek. Clove oil is used in traditional medicine as an anodyne, chiefly for dental emergencies. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> cloves were used in burial and embalming practices from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The earliest known macro-botanical cloves in northwest Europe come from the wreck of the Danish-Norwegian flagship Gribshunden, which sank in June 1495.</p><p>In the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> sought a monopoly in cloves as it had in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a>. In 1770, on the instruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-poivre">Pierre Poivre</a>, seedlings of an old tree on Ternate were taken to the Isle de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, and later spread to Martinique and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. Indonesia is today the largest producer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clove” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The spice monopolies enforced across the Maluku Islands, covering nutmeg, <b>cloves</b> and mace, were maintained through deliberate violence, including the Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands under Jan Pieterszoon Coen in 1621, with the near-extermination and enslavement of their population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its function was collection and redistribution: <b>cloves</b>, nutmeg and mace from the Maluku Islands; cotton textiles from Gujarat, the Coromandel and Malabar coasts and Bengal; porcelain, silk and iron from China; camphor, sandalwood, spices, fish roe and seaweed from the archipelago.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The spice trade was enormously profitable in the decades around 1500–1550: pepper, cinnamon, <b>cloves</b> and nutmeg generated revenues that made Lisbon one of Europe’s wealthiest cities and funded the Manueline building programme, including the Jerónimos Monastery and the Tower of Belém.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Sayyid Said bin Sultan, ruler of Oman from 1806, turned that claim into an empire: he subdued the rival Mazrui governors of Mombasa by 1837, encouraged the planting of <b>cloves</b> on Zanzibar and Pemba, and from about 1840 made Zanzibar town his principal residence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Exports have included coffee, cotton, sisal, cashew nuts, tea and Zanzibar’s <b>cloves</b>, and later gold, tourism and natural gas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 19th century the sultans of Oman moved their capital to Zanzibar, in 1840, and the island became the centre of a commercial empire built on <b>cloves</b>, ivory and the slave trade.</blockquote><a href="/language/swahili">Swahili · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
